# Recipe: a receipt

A narrow page as a receipt printer's paper roll has it, a logo, the items, totals and a QR code.
<% if example?("receipt") -%>
The example in the gem: `stationery examples receipt --source`.
<% else -%>
The examples in the gem closest to it: `stationery examples shipping_label --source` (a narrow page,
barcodes, black and white) and `stationery examples invoice --source` (items and totals).
<% end -%>

```ruby
class ReceiptPdf < Stationery::Document
  LOGO = File.expand_path("logo.png", __dir__)        # in Rails: Rails.root.join("app/assets/images/logo.png").to_s

  page size: [mm(80), mm(170)], margin: [mm(5), mm(4)] # 80 mm paper; the length is fixed, so choose it
  max_pages 1                                         # one slip: what does not fit warns (strict raises)
  default_text size: 8.5
  metadata title: "Receipt", lang: "en"

  def self.preview
    new(number: "R-1042", time: "2026-09-29 14:32",
        items: [["Flat white", 2, 4_50], ["Cinnamon bun", 1, 3_80], ["Sparkling water", 1, 2_90]])
  end

  def initialize(number:, time:, items:)
    super()
    @number = number
    @time = time
    @items = items
  end

  def view_template
    image LOGO, height: 28, align: :center
    spacer 6
    text "Harbour Café · Storgatan 1, Stockholm", align: :center
    text "Receipt #{@number} · #{@time}", size: 7.5, align: :center
    spacer 8
    rows = @items.map { |name, qty, cents| ["#{qty} × #{name}", money(qty * cents)] }
    rows << ["Total", money(total)]
    table(rows, width: :full, widths: [nil, 60], cell: { borders: [], padding: [2, 0] }) do |t|
      t.column(1).align = :right
      t.row(-1).set(weight: :bold, size: 11, borders: [:top], border_width: 1)
    end
    spacer 6
    text "VAT 12% included: #{money((total * 12 / 112.0).round)}", size: 7.5
    spacer 10
    barcode "https://harbour.example/r/#{@number}", type: :qr, module_size: 1.5, align: :center
    spacer 4
    text "Thank you!", align: :center, weight: :bold
  end

  private

  def total = @items.sum { |_, qty, cents| qty * cents }
  def money(cents) = format("%.2f", cents / 100.0)
end
```

## What matters here

- **The page is the paper**: `page size: [mm(80), mm(170)]` (or `"80mm x 170mm"`). A page has a
  fixed length, set at class level, so a receipt of any number of lines either continues on a
  second page (which `max_pages 1` reports as `Warnings::TooManyPages`, naming what moved there) or
  renders from a subclass of the length it needs, which keeps `max_pages`:
  `Class.new(ReceiptPdf) { page size: [mm(80), mm(90 + (items.size * 5))], margin: [mm(5), mm(4)] }.new(**attributes)`.
- **Totals as a table** of two columns: `widths: [nil, 60]` gives the prices 60 pt and the names
  the rest, and `cell: { borders: [] }` takes away the default borders.
- **A QR code** is `barcode data, type: :qr`; it is vector bars, so it prints sharp at any size.
- **A thermal receipt printer** prints black or nothing: `monochrome dpi: 203` reports what it
  cannot print as it is, and `monochrome dpi: 203, snap: true, dither: :threshold` makes every
  colour black or white and cuts a colour logo at half so its letters stay clean.
  `to_png(monochrome: true)` is the one-bit picture it will print. Receipt printers are driven in
  ESC/POS, which Stationery does not write: print the PDF through the printer's driver, or send the
  picture to one that takes images. `to_zpl` is for ZPL label printers (see [label.md](label.md)).
- Look at it: `stationery render receipt_pdf.rb --png` and open the picture; a narrow page shows
  where a long item name wraps. It should be one page: with `max_pages 1` a receipt that does not
  fit prints `warning: the document may have 1 page and needs 2: …` and writes a `receipt_pdf-2.png`
  of what moved. Decode the QR code from the picture: `zbarimg -q receipt_pdf-1.png`
  (`brew install zbar`) prints `QR-Code:https://…`.
